" charset="0"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="1600"/><a:t>In the AZ/NZ 1995:2002 welding cable standard, there are three tables outlining duty cycles: 30 seconds, 5 minutes, and 10 minutes.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:pPr marL="285750" indent="-285750" algn="just"><a:buFont typeface="Arial" panose="020B0604020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="1600"/><a:t> When we focus on the 30-second duty cycle for cranking, we can estimate how much current a 120 mm² cable can handle for 10 seconds.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:pPr marL="285750" indent="-285750" algn="just"><a:buFont typeface="Arial" panose="020B0604020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="1600"/><a:t> Although the table does not specify the performance at a 33% duty cycle—where the current is drawn for 10 seconds followed by a 20-second cooling period, we can interpolate the value, which is approximately 782 A. </a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:pPr marL="285750" indent="-285750" algn="just"><a:buFont typeface="Arial" panose="020B0604020202020204" pitchFamily="34" charset="0"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="1600"/><a:t>To handle 1000 A, the duty cycle required is roughly 19.37%, corresponding to a duration of about 5 seconds.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-AU" sz="1600"/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp></p:spTree><p:extLst><p:ext uri="{BB962C8B-B14F-4D97-AF65-F5344CB8AC3E}"><p14:creationId xmlns:p14="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/powerpoint/2010/main" val="1720685522"/></p:ext></p:extLst></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>